"CONSUMER CONTRACTS\ndefendants, sought to enforce their own policies, arguing that those policies constituted contracts\nand that consumers' assent to them operated as a defense against the alleged privacy violations.\nIn 35 of the 48 cases, the courts explicitly addressed the classification of privacy notices as\ncontracts, although sometimes only in dicta and concluded that privacy notices could give rise to\ncontractual obligations. In nine of those cases, the courts failed to find a valid claim for breach of\ncontract for reasons internal to contract claims, including failure of consideration or lack of\nmutuality, insufficient notice to adopt the terms, and failure to ascertain damages for breach of\ncontract. In only four cases, including the original Northwest Airlines decision, the courts decided\notherwise, concluding or implying that privacy notices are not attempts to form contracts. The\nthree additional cases in this category are Dyer v. Northwest Airlines Corporations, 334 F. Supp.\n2d 1196 (D.N.D. 2004); Lucky v. Kentucky Bank (In re Lucky), Nos. 05-54625, 10-5085 (Bankr.\nE.D. Ky. Mar. 21, 2011); and Austin-Spearman v. AARP and AARP Services Inc., 119 F. Supp.\n3d 1, 13-14 (D.D.C. 2015). The remaining cases offer further evidence of courts' willingness to\nconsider contractual enforcement of statements regarding information privacy. See, e.g., Silver v.\nStripe Inc., 2021 WL 3191752 (N.D. Cal. July 28, 2021) (holding that consumers had consented\nto the seller's privacy policy each time they placed an order, creating contractual consequences);\nIn re Marriot Int'l, Inc., Customer Data Sec. Breach Litig., 440 F. Supp. 3d 447 (D. Md. 2020)\n(denying business's motion to dismiss contract claims, and stating that plaintiffs plausibly stated\nclaims for breach of contract for alleged violations of business's privacy statements).\nThe conclusion that privacy notices can give rise to contractual obligations does not\npreclude the application of specific rules arising from privacy law. It suggests, however, that unless\na clear, overriding reason exists, the general rules and principles of this Restatement ought to apply.\nThis coexistence between this Restatement and area-specific rules applies also in other contexts.\nThis Restatement is a complementary source to statutory law operating in the area of consumer\ncontracts (e.g., the UCC, the Magnuson-Moss Warranty Act, 15 U.S.C. §§ 2301-2312, the Federal\nTrade Commission Act, 5 U.S.C. §§ 41-58, the Dodd-Frank Wall Street Reform and Consumer\nProtection Act, Pub. L. 111-203, the Federal Arbitration Act, 9 U.S.C. ch. 1, and state unfair-and-\ndeceptive-acts-and-practices statutes). Courts adjudicating contract disputes that invoke those\nstatutes have relied on, and developed, principles of consumer-contract law that extend beyond the\nstatutory framework. Those principles inform the rules restated herein.\nFinally, this Restatement's rules may be suitable for, and indeed reflect, rules being applied\nby courts in transactions other than consumer contracts. The primary criterion that requires specific\nattention to consumer contracts-the asymmetry of knowledge, sophistication, and drafting\nprowess-may similarly be present when one of the parties to the contract is not a consumer. For\nexample, it may be present in dealings between a small business and a large corporation, between\na franchisee and a franchisor, and between a platform and business user.\n24\n@ 1\n"